# Methodology

This document covers the analytical decisions behind this project, 
not just what was built, but why.

## Choosing the dataset

The goal was public, high-volume, single-business user engagement data. 
GA4's BigQuery sample export from the Google Merchandise Store fit this 
directly, official Google data, event-level granularity, and large 
enough volume (several million events across 3 months) to support 
genuine trend and cohort analysis.

## Defining the fact table

The dataset initially looks like it could support multiple fact tables, 
device, traffic, and ecommerce data all carry measures. But none of 
these have an independent grain, they only exist as attributes of a 
specific event. The test applied: does this entity have its own natural 
key and exist independently of a triggering event? Device, traffic, and 
geo failed that test and became dimensions. `items` passed it, since 
one event can contain multiple items, giving it a finer grain than the 
event table itself, so it became a second fact table.

## Surrogate keys for dimensions

Device, traffic, and geo have no natural identifiers in the GA4 export. 
Surrogate keys were generated as MD5 hashes of each dimension's 
attribute combination (e.g., device category + OS + browser), computed 
identically in both the fact table and the dimension table's SQL, so 
the values align for relationship building in Power BI.

## Deriving sessions

GA4 exports events only, there is no native session table. Session-level 
metrics (duration, event count, bounce flag) were built by aggregating 
events on `user_pseudo_id` + `ga_session_id`, extracted from the nested 
`event_params` array. A session's duration is measured as the time 
between its first and last recorded event, not a true "session close" 
signal, meaning single-event sessions register as zero-duration by 
construction. This is a known limitation of deriving duration this way, 
not a data error.

## Dropping repeat-purchase analysis

An early design question was: how many sessions occur between a user's 
purchases? A volume check showed fewer than 20% of purchasing users 
make more than one purchase within the 3-month window, too sparse a 
base for a reliable repeat-purchase metric. This was replaced with a 
purchase funnel (view → cart → checkout → purchase) as a more robust 
way to study conversion behavior with the available data.

A related metric, average sessions per purchase, was built instead: 
for each purchasing user, total sessions up to their last purchase 
divided by their total purchase count. Unlike a gap-between-purchases 
metric, this returns a value for every purchasing user, including 
one-time buyers, since it measures sessions-to-conversion rather than 
a repeat-purchase cadence.

## Funnel definition

The purchase funnel includes `view_item`, `add_to_cart`, `begin_checkout`, 
`add_shipping_info`, `add_payment_info`, and `purchase`. This mirrors 
GA4's own ecommerce event structure rather than an arbitrary set of 
milestones, so drop-off at each step reflects an actual step in the 
site's checkout flow. A parallel item-level funnel was also built, 
tracking unit quantities (not sessions) through the same stages, since 
session-based and unit-based funnels can diverge, for example, a few 
users adding many units of one item will move the item funnel 
differently than the session funnel, which counts users only once 
regardless of quantity.

## New vs. returning users

Defined using GA4's `ga_session_number` parameter: a session with 
`session_number = 1` is a user's first-ever session (new), anything 
greater is returning. This was chosen over a "first appearance that 
day" definition since it reflects genuine lifetime user status rather 
than daily novelty.

## Known limitations

- Session duration is bounded by GA4's tracked event timestamps, not a 
  true session-close event, so very short (single-event) sessions show 
  zero duration
- `user_properties` was checked and found to be essentially unpopulated 
  in this sample dataset, and was excluded from the model
- The dataset's three-month window limits longer-term cohort or 
  seasonality analysis
